<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick Jackson Turner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick Jackson Turner was an American historian, based at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> until 1910 and then at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a>, known primarily for his frontier thesis. Born in Portage, Wisconsin, he earned his PhD at Johns Hopkins University in 1890 with a thesis on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> in Wisconsin. His essay The Significance of the Frontier in American History argued that the moving western frontier shaped American democracy and character from the colonial era until 1890. He published little but trained many historians, and by his death in 1932, 60% of the major history <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> in the U.S. taught frontier history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-jackson-turner.jpg" alt="Frederick Jackson Turner" width="330" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frederick_Jackson_Turner_1911.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Turner was the son of Andrew Jackson Turner, a Republican newspaper editor and publisher who invested in a railroad, and Mary Olivia Hanford Turner, a schoolteacher. He was influenced by the writing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-waldo-emerson">Ralph Waldo Emerson</a> and by scientists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-spencer">Herbert Spencer</a>. He graduated from the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> in 1884. His doctoral thesis at Johns Hopkins University, supervised by Herbert Baxter Adams, was titled The Character and Influence of the Indian Trade in Wisconsin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Turner did not publish extensively: his influence came from tersely expressed interpretive theories, chiefly the Frontier Thesis and the Sectional Hypothesis. By 1910 he was reckoned one of the two or three most influential historians in the country. He found posts in major universities for his students, such as Merle Curti and Marcus Lee Hansen. Annoyed by regents who demanded less research and more teaching, he accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a> in 1910 and stayed until 1922, then became a visiting scholar at the Huntington Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/los-angeles">Los Angeles</a>. The Frontier in American History of 1920 was a collection of older essays.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The frontier thesis was set out in a paper of 1893, read before the American Historical Association during the World's Columbian Exposition in Chicago. Turner argued that the moving western frontier exerted a strong influence on American democracy and character: each generation of pioneers moved 50 to 100 miles west, left behind European practices and found new solutions, producing informality, violence, crudeness, democracy and initiative. His essays on sectionalism, collected in The Significance of Sections in American History, won the Pulitzer Prize in History in 1933.</p><p>His theories became unfashionable in the 1960s. Critics complained that he neglected regionalism, ignored gender and gave too little weight to class. Isaiah Bowman said that his ideas were curiously wanting in evidence from field studies. Turner died in 1932 in Pasadena, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, and the Organization of American Historians gives the Frederick Jackson Turner Award for an author's first scholarly book on American history.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick Jackson Turner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
